{"id": "seed-cmp-04", "category": "comparison", "prompt_version": 2, "requested_model": "opus", "cli_version": "2.1.302 (Claude Code)", "a": 5.0, "b": 5.0, "diff": 0.0, "outcome": "tie", "identical": false, "a_criteria": {"correctness": 2.0, "completeness": 2.0, "groundedness": 1.0, "usefulness": 0.0}, "b_criteria": {"correctness": 2.0, "completeness": 2.0, "groundedness": 1.0, "usefulness": 0.0}, "orders": [{"a_pos": "A", "a": {"correctness": 2, "completeness": 2, "groundedness": 1, "usefulness": 0}, "b": {"correctness": 2, "completeness": 2, "groundedness": 1, "usefulness": 0}, "preferred": "tie", "rationale": "The two answers have the same content and differ only in how NaHCO3 is formatted, so it's a tie. Both misstate the chemistry: heating baking soda alone gives sodium carbonate, and baking powder's acid avoids that. Both also wrongly say soda is \"mainly\" used to tenderize meat. Neither clearly says baking soda needs an acid from the recipe.", "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "models_used": ["claude-opus-5-5"], "cost_usd": 0.031627999999999996, "duration_ms": 5517, "num_turns": 2, "web_search_requests": 0}, {"a_pos": "B", "a": {"correctness": 2, "completeness": 2, "groundedness": 1, "usefulness": 0}, "b": {"correctness": 2, "completeness": 2, "groundedness": 1, "usefulness": 0}, "preferred": "tie", "rationale": "The answers make identical claims and differ only in how they format NaHCO3. Both wrongly say baking powder's acid creates sodium carbonate; heat does that. Both overstate the Chinese-cuisine use as baking soda's main one. Neither says plainly that baking soda needs an acidic ingredient while baking powder contains its own acid. Tie.", "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "models_used": ["claude-opus-5-5"], "cost_usd": 0.0299484, "duration_ms": 8757, "num_turns": 2, "web_search_requests": 0}]}
